    "correct_answer_latex": "$\\frac{5}{6}r$",
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "solution_latex": "Entering corner splits current into 3 symmetric branches ($I/3$ each). Each branch splits into 2 ($I/6$). Finally they recombine in 3 branches ($I/3$). Total voltage drop $V = \\frac{I}{3}r + \\frac{I}{6}r + \\frac{I}{3}r = \\left(\\frac{2}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{2}{6}\\right)Ir = \\frac{5}{6}Ir$. Equivalent resistance $R_{\\text{eq}} = V/I = \\frac{5}{6}r$.",
    "socratic_hints": [
      "Use symmetry of current entering diagonally opposite corners.",
      "Current splits into 3 edges ($I/3$), then 6 edges ($I/6$), then 3 edges ($I/3$).",
      "Sum potential drops: $(1/3 + 1/6 + 1/3)r = 5/6 r$."
    ],
